<ChapterLayout
  title="4.1 — The Solar System · AST 100"
  description="Our home system from the inside out — rocky planets, asteroid belt, gas and ice giants, Kuiper belt, heliosphere, and the Oort cloud — plus the tilts, spins, and interiors of the eight planets."
  activeId="4.1" chapter={4} current="/chapter/4/4.1">
  <Hero
    tease="Our home system, mapped from the inside out. Eight planets ride one flat disk around an ordinary star — rocky worlds close in, giants beyond — but the system does not end at Neptune: past it lie the icy Kuiper belt, the vast bubble blown by the solar wind, and finally the Oort cloud, a sphere of comets reaching a third of the way to the next star."
    client:load />

  <article class="topic-page">
    <div class="prose-cosmic lesson-prose">
      <h2 id="architecture">One System, Four Scales</h2>
      <p>
        The Stellar Age of <a href={subPath("3.0")}>chapter 3</a> ended close to home, with the cloud that collapsed to
        make the Sun. This chapter stays home and asks what came with it — because a star is rarely born alone. Around
        our ordinary yellow star (<a href={subPath("3.1")}>§3.1</a>) orbits a whole household of worlds: eight planets,
        their moons, and countless smaller bodies, all bound by the Sun's gravity. That household is the
        <strong>solar system</strong>, and its most striking feature is its shape. Almost everything in it moves in the
        same direction around the Sun, in nearly the same flat plane — a plane called the <strong>ecliptic</strong>. The
        solar system is, to a very good approximation, a disk. Why it is a disk is the story of
        <a href={subPath("4.3")}>§4.3</a>; here we first walk the disk from the inside out.
      </p>
      <p>
        Closest to the Sun, within about one and a half times the Earth–Sun distance, sit the four small rocky worlds:
        <strong>Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars</strong>. Astronomers measure these distances in
        <strong>astronomical units</strong> (au) — one au is the average Earth–Sun distance, about 150 million
        kilometres. Beyond Mars, between roughly 2 and 3 au, circles the <strong>asteroid belt</strong>: rocky fragments
        that never gathered into a planet. Two further swarms of asteroids, the <strong>Trojans</strong>, share
        Jupiter's own orbit, held 60° ahead of and behind the planet by a gravitational balancing act between Jupiter
        and the Sun. Then come the four giants — <strong>Jupiter and Saturn</strong>, made mostly of gas, and
        <strong>Uranus and Neptune</strong>, the ice giants — spread from 5 to 30 au. Past Neptune the disk continues as
        the <strong>Kuiper belt</strong>, a ring of icy leftover bodies between about 30 and 50 au that includes the
        dwarf planet Pluto.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Sun's influence reaches much farther than its planets. From its outer atmosphere — the corona of
        <a href={subPath("3.1")}>§3.1</a> — the Sun constantly exhales a thin, fast stream of charged particles called
        the <strong>solar wind</strong>. This wind inflates an enormous bubble in the gas between the stars, the
        <strong>heliosphere</strong>. Where the wind finally slows against the interstellar gas, at about 94 au, is the
        <strong>termination shock</strong>; where its push is fully balanced and interstellar space truly begins, at
        roughly 122 au, is the <strong>heliopause</strong>. This is not a theoretical boundary: the
        <strong>Voyager 1</strong> spacecraft, launched in 1977, crossed it in August 2012 and became humanity's first
        interstellar probe. And because the whole bubble is flying through the galaxy, it is not a neat sphere — the
        gas ahead piles up while the bubble trails a long <strong>heliotail</strong> behind, like the wake of a ship.
      </p>
      <p>
        Even the heliopause is not the edge. The true frontier of the Sun's gravitational dominance is the
        <strong>Oort cloud</strong> — a spherical shell of billions of icy bodies, leftover building blocks flung
        outward during the system's violent youth. It is thought to begin a few thousand au out and reach to about
        <strong>100,000 au — roughly 1.6 light-years</strong>, a third of the way to the nearest star. Unlike the
        planets and the Kuiper belt, the Oort cloud is not a disk at all but a three-dimensional sphere enclosing
        everything, and it is where the long-period comets come from. Step outward through the four frames of the
        figure below — each one a wider view of the same system — using the arrow keys.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="The solar system, four frames out">
        <ArchitecturePanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <h2 id="worlds">Eight Worlds, One Disk</h2>
      <p>
        Within that architecture, every planet performs the same dual motion: it <strong>revolves</strong> around the
        Sun along the shared disk, and it <strong>rotates</strong> — spins — on its own axis. The revolutions are
        orderly; the rotations are anything but. Each planet's spin axis leans at its own angle away from "upright,"
        an angle called the <strong>axial tilt</strong>. That single number shapes a world's whole calendar, because
        it sets how directly sunlight strikes each hemisphere through the year: tilt is what makes
        <strong>seasons</strong>. Earth's familiar 23.4° lean gives us ours. Pick each planet in the figure below —
        arrow keys work — and watch the axis swing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The extremes are wonderfully strange. Mercury stands almost perfectly upright (0.03°) and has essentially no
        seasons. <strong>Venus is tipped 177°</strong> — flipped nearly upside down — so its spin runs backwards:
        on Venus the Sun rises in the west and sets in the east, and one lazy rotation takes 243 Earth days, longer
        than its year. <strong>Uranus lies at 98°</strong>, rolling on its side, so each pole enjoys about 42 years of
        unbroken sunlight while the other endures decades of frozen dark. Pluto, tipped 122.5°, spins backwards too.
        These odd tilts are not decoration — they are <em>evidence</em>, the surviving scars of the collisions and
        gravitational shoves that battered the young solar system, a violence we return to in
        <a href={subPath("4.3")}>§4.3</a>.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Tilt and spin of the nine worlds">
        <TiltSpinPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        Cut the planets open and the eight worlds sort themselves into families — the same families their positions in
        the disk already hinted at. The four <strong>terrestrial</strong> (rocky) planets all repeat one recipe: a
        dense <strong>core of iron and nickel</strong>, wrapped in a thick mantle of silicate rock, capped by a thin
        solid crust. The proportions vary tellingly. Mercury's metal core fills about 83% of its radius — evidence
        that a giant early impact stripped away much of its rock — while Earth and Venus are more balanced, and
        Earth's core is split into a solid inner ball and a churning liquid shell (which, as
        <a href={subPath("4.2")}>§4.2</a> shows, is what generates our magnetic shield).
      </p>
      <p>
        The giants are built on an entirely different plan: they have no surface to stand on. <strong>Jupiter and
        Saturn</strong> are almost all hydrogen — an atmosphere thousands of kilometres deep that grades into liquid,
        and then, under pressures millions of times Earth's, into an exotic electricity-conducting state called
        <strong>metallic hydrogen</strong> — around a comparatively small core of rock and iron.
        <strong>Uranus and Neptune</strong> carry a thinner hydrogen coat over a deep, hot fluid mantle of water,
        ammonia, and methane — compounds astronomers collectively call "ices," which is why these two are the
        <strong>ice giants</strong>. Step through all eight interiors below; the strip along the bottom keeps the true
        size comparison honest — Jupiter is eleven Earths across. Why the rocky worlds formed close in and the giants
        far out is exactly where <a href={subPath("4.3")}>§4.3</a> picks up.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Interiors of the eight planets">
        <InteriorsPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <hr />

      <p><em>One ordinary star; a flat disk of eight worlds, two belts of rubble and ice; a wind-blown bubble reaching
        122 au; and a spherical cloud of comets stretching a third of the way to the next star. That is the estate.
        Next we sort its worlds — and thousands found around other stars — into types.</em></p>
    </div>
